# W4-SETTLEMENT-1 W5 Proof B: existing-town settlement activity observable through the real
# developer inspection UI. Builds a pioneer house (type 501, the id population_progression_test.cpp
# and opening_resume.script already use) through the real dev tool on the lan5 developer island,
# then reads the developer inspection UI's facts for the hovered tile and confirms they show real,
# live household state (residents, need stock) read straight from the sim's Payload, not a static
# placeholder. Launch: --play data/maps/lan5.txt (the default).
#
# This script intentionally stops at household state, not active production: building a producing
# workshop or farm at this exact spot was tried and found blocked (MissingInput: no road-connected
# warehouse delivery and no adjacent crop field at this tile) — the legal supply chain (roads, field
# adjacency) that would unblock it is MAP-TOWN-1/MAP-NEEDS-2 territory (queued, not released;
# docs/orchestration/gameplay-next-chain.md), not this package's presentation-layer scope.
look 1140 516
key b
select 501
rotate 0
wait frames 60
click 1156 516
wait outcome
wait turns 5
expect fact inspection.kind == House
# "contains residents=5" alone also matches "residents=50"; House's status (play.cpp's
# DescribePayload) always follows the residents count with " stock=", so matching through that
# token pins the digit count exactly (W4-SETTLEMENT-1 W7 review, finding 9).
expect fact inspection.status contains residents=5 stock=
expect fact inspection.type == 501
# Negative step (finding 3/9, W4-MAIN-2 part S finding 2): pausing stops new turns from
# publishing, so this is the one case that actually exercises live_inspection's hover check
# rather than the sim naturally catching up. Hovering empty ground at the same zoom (confirmed
# building-free by probing the real map) must clear the line at once, not keep showing the house
# from before the hover moved. This must be a hover, not a click: the developer building tool is
# still on with type 501 selected (the `select 501` above), so a `click` here would queue a Build
# at this tile instead of testing the inspection line. `look` is the script language's plain
# pointer-move verb (used for the first hover above too) - it sets the hover tile without
# synthesizing a mouse button event.
key space
wait frames 1
look 1130 516
wait frames 1
expect fact inspection.kind != House
quit
